14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 1861–March 8, 1862","place":"Gosport Navy Yard, Norfolk, and Hampton Roads, Virginia","result":"The Confederacy rebuilt the burned steam frigate USS Merrimack as the ironclad CSS Virginia. On March 8, 1862, Franklin Buchanan took Virginia into Hampton Roads and attacked the United States blockading squadron, sinking USS Cumberland and destroying USS Congress. USS Monitor arrived that night; the duel between the ironclads is covered in episode 106","facts":[{"label":"CSS Virginia commissioned","value":"February 17, 1862"},{"label":"Hull","value":"275 feet long; slightly more than 51 feet wide"},{"label":"Crew","value":"About 320 officers and men"},{"label":"Casemate protection","value":"24 inches of pine and oak under two layers of 2-inch iron plate"},{"label":"Ram","value":"About 1,500 pounds and projected roughly 3 feet from the bow"},{"label":"Armament","value":"Two 7-inch Brooke rifles, two 6.4-inch Brooke rifles, and six 9-inch Dahlgren smoothbores"},{"label":"Maximum speed","value":"About 5–6 knots with unreliable engines"},{"label":"March 8 result","value":"Cumberland sunk; Congress destroyed; Minnesota grounded; Virginia withdrew with the tide"}],"maps":[{"title":"Hampton Roads today","caption":"Hampton Roads is the harbor where the James, Elizabeth, and Nansemond rivers meet the Chesapeake Bay. The former Gosport Navy Yard is now Norfolk Naval Shipyard in Portsmouth. Newport News Point, Sewell’s Point, and Fort Monroe define the approaches to the 1862 battlefield.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-76.48%2C36.76%2C-76.20%2C37.05&layer=mapnik&marker=36.987%2C-76.309"},{"title":"Virginia’s route and the blockading squadron","caption":"Red shows CSS Virginia leaving Gosport Navy Yard and moving toward the United States ships off Newport News on March 8. Blue marks Cumberland, Congress, and the grounded Minnesota. Monitor arrived from the Chesapeake that night.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html","image":"/images/hampton-roads-march-8-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Hampton Roads showing CSS Virginia moving from Gosport Navy Yard toward USS Cumberland and USS Congress off Newport News"},{"title":"Hampton Roads and enemy positions in 1862","caption":"This wartime U.S. Army reconnaissance map shows the Elizabeth River, Norfolk and Portsmouth, Newport News, Fort Monroe, and military positions around Hampton Roads.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html","image":"/images/hampton-roads-1862-map.jpg","alt":"1862 U.S. Army reconnaissance map of the Hampton Roads vicinity with military positions"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:08","audioTime":68,"title":"The naval battle coincided with Pea Ridge","detail":"On March 8, 1862, the first day of the Hampton Roads battle began while the armies at Pea Ridge were preparing for their second day of fighting."},{"time":"1:19","audioTime":79,"title":"Confederates recovered Merrimack’s hull","detail":"United States forces burned and sank USS Merrimack when they evacuated Gosport Navy Yard in April 1861. The machinery and lower hull remained usable after the Confederacy took the yard."},{"time":"1:46","audioTime":106,"title":"Merrimack became CSS Virginia","detail":"Confederate engineers cut down the frigate and built an armored casemate over the surviving hull. The completed ship was commissioned as CSS Virginia."},{"time":"1:50","audioTime":110,"title":"Wood and iron protected the casemate","detail":"The sloped sides used 24 inches of pine and oak covered by two layers of 2-inch iron plate."},{"time":"2:02","audioTime":122,"title":"A ram was fixed to the bow","detail":"A roughly 1,500-pound iron ram projected about three feet from the bow for attacks below an enemy ship’s waterline."},{"time":"2:04","audioTime":124,"title":"Virginia was a large ship","detail":"The rebuilt vessel was 275 feet long, slightly more than 51 feet wide, and carried a crew of about 320."},{"time":"2:13","audioTime":133,"title":"Ten guns filled the battery","detail":"Virginia carried two 7-inch Brooke rifles, two 6.4-inch Brooke rifles, and six 9-inch Dahlgren smoothbores."},{"time":"2:25","audioTime":145,"title":"Virginia entered Confederate service","detail":"The Confederate Navy commissioned the ship on February 17, 1862."},{"time":"2:31","audioTime":151,"title":"Buchanan left Gosport on March 8","detail":"Franklin Buchanan took Virginia down the Elizabeth River on what was expected to be a trial run, then continued into Hampton Roads to attack the blockading squadron."},{"time":"2:49","audioTime":169,"title":"Virginia broke the wooden-ship line","detail":"Virginia sank Cumberland with gunfire and its ram, forced Congress to surrender and burn, and helped drive Minnesota, Roanoke, and St. Lawrence aground."},{"time":"3:10","audioTime":190,"title":"Virginia withdrew at about 5 p.m.","detail":"The falling tide and damage to the ship led Buchanan’s command to leave the scene and return toward the Elizabeth River."},{"time":"3:27","audioTime":207,"title":"Monitor reached Hampton Roads that night","detail":"USS Monitor arrived from New York and took position near the grounded Minnesota. The ironclads fought on March 9."},{"time":"4:23","audioTime":263,"title":"Stephen Mallory became Confederate Navy secretary","detail":"Jefferson Davis appointed Mallory in February 1861. The Confederacy began without a national navy or a shipbuilding system."},{"time":"5:07","audioTime":307,"title":"Mallory had maritime and Senate experience","detail":"Mallory was born in Trinidad, grew up in Key West, practiced maritime law, and won election to the United States Senate in 1851."},{"time":"6:25","audioTime":385,"title":"Mallory had directed naval legislation","detail":"As a member and later chairman of the Senate Naval Affairs Committee, he supported fleet expansion and technical improvements before secession."},{"time":"7:43","audioTime":463,"title":"The Confederacy started with ten small vessels","detail":"Its initial force consisted of four revenue cutters, an old side-wheel steamer, and several tenders and tugs. None could match a major United States warship."},{"time":"9:30","audioTime":570,"title":"The United States fleet expanded rapidly","detail":"The United States Navy had more than 100 warships early in the conflict and 264 by December 1861."},{"time":"10:22","audioTime":622,"title":"Mallory chose several ways to offset the fleet imbalance","detail":"His department pursued new weapons, bought and built ships abroad, and used commerce raiders and privateers against United States shipping."},{"time":"12:44","audioTime":764,"title":"The Confederate Navy developed new weapons","detail":"The department supported electrically detonated mines, then called torpedoes, as well as submarines and armored ships."},{"time":"13:29","audioTime":809,"title":"Mallory made iron armor a priority","detail":"He argued that armored ships could withstand the guns of wooden warships and reduce the United States Navy’s numerical advantage."},{"time":"15:23","audioTime":923,"title":"Armored vessels already existed","detail":"Korean, Spanish, French, and British ships had used armor before the Civil War. The French Gloire and British Warrior showed that seagoing armored warships were practical."},{"time":"18:23","audioTime":1103,"title":"The design team selected Merrimack","detail":"John M. Brooke, John L. Porter, and William P. Williamson met Mallory on June 23, 1861, and recommended converting the wreck at Gosport."},{"time":"19:12","audioTime":1152,"title":"Congress funded the conversion","detail":"The Confederate Congress appropriated $170,000 for the project."},{"time":"20:08","audioTime":1208,"title":"Tredegar supplied the armor","detail":"Tredegar Iron Works in Richmond was the only Southern mill able to roll the required plates. It delivered about 725 tons of iron for the ship."},{"time":"21:24","audioTime":1284,"title":"Conditions aboard were poor","detail":"The casemate had limited ventilation, heat, smoke, and crowded accommodations. Catesby ap Roger Jones described the ship as unhealthy for its crew."},{"time":"21:56","audioTime":1316,"title":"Draft and engines limited the ship","detail":"Virginia drew more than 22 feet of water and used the unreliable engines condemned before Merrimack’s loss. It made only about five or six knots and turned slowly."},{"time":"23:10","audioTime":1390,"title":"Buchanan entered Confederate service","detail":"The veteran United States Navy officer resigned in 1861 when he expected Maryland to secede. Gideon Welles refused his attempt to withdraw the resignation, and Buchanan later joined the Confederate Navy."},{"time":"24:31","audioTime":1471,"title":"The ship went directly into combat","detail":"Buchanan reported aboard on March 4. Four days later, Virginia left on its first powered trip with untested guns and yard workers still aboard, then attacked the blockade."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Abraham Lincoln","command":"President of the United States","subordinates":"Directed the national war effort"},{"commander":"Gideon Welles","command":"Secretary of the Navy","subordinates":"Oversaw the expanding United States fleet and the blockade"},{"commander":"Louis M. Goldsborough","command":"North Atlantic Blockading Squadron","subordinates":"Senior naval commander responsible for the Hampton Roads blockade"},{"commander":"John L. Worden","command":"USS Monitor","subordinates":"Arrived in Hampton Roads during the night of March 8; the duel is covered in episode 106"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Jefferson Davis","command":"President of the Confederate States","subordinates":"Appointed the cabinet and approved naval policy"},{"commander":"Stephen R. Mallory","command":"Secretary of the Navy","subordinates":"Set strategy, supported ironclad construction, and selected the Merrimack conversion"},{"commander":"Franklin Buchanan","command":"CSS Virginia","subordinates":"Commanded the March 8 attack; Catesby ap Roger Jones served as executive officer"},{"commander":"John M. Brooke, John L. Porter, and William P. Williamson","command":"Virginia design and engineering team","subordinates":"Brooke worked on armor and ordnance, Porter on naval construction, and Williamson on machinery"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Stephen R. Mallory","role":"Confederate secretary of the navy who made armored warships central to Confederate naval strategy","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Franklin Buchanan","role":"Commanded CSS Virginia during its first combat operation on March 8","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Catesby ap Roger Jones","role":"Virginia’s executive officer and the commander who took over after Buchanan was wounded","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John M. Brooke","role":"Developed the ironclad proposal, armor arrangement, and Brooke rifled guns","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John L. Porter","role":"Naval constructor responsible for much of Virginia’s design and conversion","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William P. Williamson","role":"Chief engineer who evaluated and adapted Merrimack’s machinery","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Gideon Welles","role":"United States secretary of the navy who directed the blockade and fleet expansion","side":"United States"},{"name":"John L. Worden","role":"Commanding officer of USS Monitor, which arrived after the March 8 fighting","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Franklin Buchanan","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commanding officer of CSS Virginia","outcome":"Wounded in the thigh by rifle fire while directing the attack on USS Congress. Catesby ap Roger Jones took command."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/uss-merrimack-drawing.jpg","alt":"Drawing of the steam frigate USS Merrimack before its conversion","caption":"USS Merrimack as a steam frigate. United States forces burned and sank the ship at Gosport in April 1861, but the Confederacy recovered the lower hull and machinery.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"image":"/images/css-virginia-drydock.jpg","alt":"CSS Virginia in dry dock at Gosport Navy Yard in about February 1862","caption":"CSS Virginia in dry dock at Gosport Navy Yard around February 1862. The sloped casemate covered most of the surviving Merrimack hull.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"image":"/images/hampton-roads-ironclads-print.jpg","alt":"Lithograph showing USS Monitor and CSS Virginia fighting in Hampton Roads","caption":"A later lithograph of Monitor and Virginia. The March 9 duel follows the construction and March 8 attack covered in this episode.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/91482238/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 105","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/105-clash-of-the-ironclads-part-the-first"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: Hampton Roads","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: CSS Virginia ship history","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/research/histories/ship-histories/confederate_ships/virginia.html"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Hampton Roads","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va008"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of the Ironclads","url":"https://home.nps.gov/articles/battle-of-the-ironclads.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Monitor and Merrimac lithograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/91482238/"}]}}]}]
